WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.600
The client who wants to fine-tune.

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.748
They're paying you, and they're attached to the idea.

3
00:00:05.748 --> 00:00:08.167
How do you redirect without dodging the work?

4
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:14.067
A client insists on fine tuning a model on their five hundred support tickets.

5
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.867
They've budgeted for GPUs and expect a custom model.

6
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:21.800
You suspect what they really need is retrieval and a better prompt.

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.933
Technically, there are two different things a model could learn.

8
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:28.620
Fine tuning teaches style and format.

9
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.233
Retrieval supplies facts.

10
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.500
Five hundred tickets can teach tone, but not facts.

11
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.837
And their policies change weekly.

12
00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:39.233
Fine tuning can't keep up, retrieval updates instantly.

13
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:43.233
Fine tuning is for how you talk, retrieval is for what you know.

14
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:45.567
Now the redirect.

15
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.500
Validate first, then reframe.

16
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.552
You're right that the model needs to know your business.

17
00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:51.633
That's the whole game.

18
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:55.173
There are two things it could learn, and each has its own tool.

19
00:00:55.173 --> 00:00:58.567
You're not saying no, you're showing which tool serves which part.

20
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.633
Then propose something empirical.

21
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:05.733
Two weeks of RAG over their ticket history and policy documents.

22
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:08.867
With an eval set built from a hundred real tickets.

23
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.667
Measure the answer quality.

24
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:15.867
If the gap that remains is tone and format, that's exactly what a fine tune fixes.

25
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:19.658
And you'd do it with better data, because you know the target.

26
00:01:19.658 --> 00:01:21.967
Fine tuning second is cheaper and better.

27
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:26.881
One trap is building what they asked for just because they pay.

28
00:01:26.881 --> 00:01:30.300
Six weeks later it still doesn't know last month's policy.

29
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.417
The other is being dismissive.

30
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:34.900
Instead, measure, and let the data speak.

31
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:36.908
Let's recap.

32
00:01:36.908 --> 00:01:39.600
Fine tune for style, retrieve for facts.

33
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.833
Validate the goal, then reframe.

34
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:43.700
Prove it with a two week eval.

35
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.506
Fine tune second, with better data.

36
00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:50.300
Next up: the four hundred page PDF nobody can search.
